# Starter .gitignore for a research analysis project (R, Python, Quarto).
# Read the comments and delete what does not apply. Rules are ordered: later lines win.

# --- R and RStudio ---
.Rproj.user/
.Rhistory
.RData
.Ruserdata
*_cache/
# renv writes its own renv/.gitignore (library/ is ignored there); renv.lock and renv/activate.R are tracked

# --- Python ---
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
# uv.lock and pyproject.toml are tracked

# --- Regenerable pipeline and render output (rebuild with the pipeline) ---
_targets/
.snakemake/
/.quarto/
/_site/
*_files/
# _freeze/ is deliberately NOT ignored: Quarto recommends committing it when rendering is expensive

# --- Secrets: never commit; rotate anything that was ---
.env
.Renviron
.netrc
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

# --- OS and editor files ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/

# --- Lock files from tools: only the pipeline lockfiles are wanted ---
# Do NOT use a bare `*.lock` here: it also hides renv.lock and uv.lock.
# If you must, add the two negations right below it:
# *.lock
# !renv.lock
# !uv.lock

# --- Data: originals stay on the server; Git keeps a manifest, not the files ---
# Write `data/*`, never `data/`: a negation under an excluded directory does nothing.
data/*
!data/README.md
!data/manifest.tsv
# Small input tables that ARE meant to be tracked can get their own negation, e.g.:
# !data/lookup_species.csv
